# function Emitter(this: EmitterLike) { this.capture = false }   function mark(emitter: EmitterLike) { emitter.marked = 2 }
# 70 literals of 70 layouts are EmitterLike, and unknown code has them all, with both functions.
# A name that is stored through a declared receiver gets a field in the layouts that the type lists. Where they are too many to give each of them room (MaxValueFlowMembersToldApart = 64), it gets one
# only where the program names it through a type of few layouts: nothing names `marked` in that way, so it has no field.
# What a constructor function stores into its own `this` it stores into every object that passes it: `EventEmitter.call(this)` runs for the objects of a hundred classes. So `capture` gets its field in each,
# and in the layout of the objects that `new Emitter()` makes: only unknown code constructs with the function here, so its `this` is an object that nobody has followed and no value is listed under the name
# LOSS: MaxValueFlowMembersToldApart x1 L0
# RUN: valueflow-apply
# CHECK: layout L0 name="Made0" fields=[{key="flags"
# CHECK-NOT: key="marked"
# CHECK: {key="capture" {{*}} flags=[IsAddedLate
# CHECK-NOT: key="marked"
# CHECK: layout L69 name="Made69" fields=[{key="flags"
# CHECK-NOT: key="marked"
# CHECK: {key="capture" {{*}} flags=[IsAddedLate
# CHECK-NOT: key="marked"
# CHECK: layout L70 name="EmitterLike"
# CHECK-NOT: IsAddedLate
# CHECK: layout L71 kind=Instance name="Emitter" constructor=f1 fields=[{key="capture" {{*}} flags=[IsAddedLate
# CHECK-NOT: key="marked"
# CHECK: function f0

